,"H1980":"went","H1659":"to grope","H5787":"blind","H7023":"wall","H5869":"eye","H3782":"to stumble","H6672":"midday","H5399":"twilight","H820":"strong","H1993":"to roar","H1677":"bear","H3123":"dove","H3444":"salvation","H7235":"to multiply","H6588":"transgression","H5048":"before","H6030":"to dwell","H854":"with","H6586":"to transgress","H3584":"to deceive","H5253":"to remove","H310":"after","H6233":"oppression","H5627":"revolt","H3820":"heart","H1697":"word","H268":"back","H7350":"distant","H5975":"stand","H7339":"street/plaza","H571":"faithful","H5229":"upright","H3201":"be able","H935":"come","H5737":"to help","H5493":"remove","H7997":"to take","H7200":"see","H7489":"be evil","H376":"man","H8074":"destroyed","H6293":"to fall on","H2220":"arm","H1931":"he/she/it","H5564":"to support","H3847":"to clothe","H8302":"lance","H3553":"helmet","H7218":"head","H5359":"vengeance","H8516":"clothing","H5844":"to enwrap","H4598":"robe","H7068":"jealousy","H1578":"recompense","H7999":"to complete","H2534":"rage","H6862":"narrow","H1576":"recompense","H341":"enemy","H339":"coastland","H3372":"to fear","H4628":"west","H853":"[Obj.]","H8034":"name","H4217":"East (Gate)","H8121":"sun","H3519":"glory","H5104":"River","H7307":"spirit","H5127":"to flee","H6726":"Zion","H1350":"redeem","H7725":"return","H3290":"Jacob","H5002":"utterance","H589":"I","H2063":"this","H1285":"covenant","H559":"to say","H834":"which","H7760":"make","H6310":"lip","H4185":"to remove","H2233":"seed","H6258":"now","H5704":"till","H5769":"enduring"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Iniquities. The history of Susanna shews that the captives were not all free from sin, which alone prevented their liberation, Lamentations iii. 44. (Calmet) [Daniel xiii.] — God is willing and able to save. He punishes for sin, to cause us to repent, ver. 20. (Worthington)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Justice. They arraign unjustly. None call upon the just God, but trust in idols. — Iniquity. They kill themselves, while they strive to injure others, Psalm vii. 15., and Micheas ii. 1."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Basilisk, or viper. (Calmet) — The young ones “burst through the viper’s sides.” (Pliny, [Natural History?] x. 62.) — So the works of the wicked are useless or destructive."],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Peace, or prosperity. They quarrel with all, and ruin themselves, Psalm xiii."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Therefore. The wicked Jews nevertheless confess that their sins prove their destruction."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Dead. The Jews will not recognize Christ, notwithstanding the prophecies and miracles."],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Judgment, that God would avenge us, (ver. 9.) and regard our fasts, chap. lviii. 3."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","In. Where truth is disregarded, there can be no justice."],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Himself, to arrest his arm, stretched out to chastise his son; or to second him. There is nothing in man to stop God’s vengeance. He therefore pardons out of his own goodness, chap. lix. 2., and lxiii. 4. (Calmet) — He became man to redeem us, as no pure mortal could do it. (Worthington)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Justice. None can blame his conduct."],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","On. Hebrew, “is standard-bearer.” (Aquila) (St. Jerome) — Cyrus, the figurative redeemer, proceeds rapidly."],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","To Sion. Septuagint, “from Sion, and will turn away iniquity from Jacob. (21.) And this,” &c. (Haydock) — St. Paul hence proves that the Jews will at last be converted, Romans xi. 26. The return of the captives prefigured this event. (Calmet)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Covenant. Note here a clear promise of perpetual orthodoxy to the Church of Christ. (Challoner) — She hath still the spirit of truth. (Worthington) (Matthew xxviii. 20.) — None will apply this to the synagogue, which is visibly in the dark, and abandoned. (Calmet)"],["challoner",21,null,"challoner","This is my covenant, etc.... Note here a clear promise of perpetual orthodoxy to the church of Christ."]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"]},"fa":[[2,null,0,"Homilies on Ephesians","Homily 5",0,407,"What the middle wall of partition is, he interprets by saying, \"the enmity having abolished in His flesh, even the law of commandments contained in ordinances. Some indeed affirm that he means the wall of the Jews against the Greeks, because it did not allow the Jews to hold intercourse with the Greeks. To me, however, this does not seem to be the meaning, but rather that he calls \"the enmity in the flesh,\" a middle wall, in that it is a common barrier, cutting us off alike from God. As the Prophet says, \"Your iniquities separate between you and Me;\" for that enmity which He had both against Jews and Gentiles was, as it were, a middle wall. …"],[2,null,0,"Homilies Concerning the Statues","Homily 6",1,407,"It is the greatest punishment to commit sin, though we may remain unpunished; as on the other hand, it is the greatest honour and repose to live virtuously, though we may be punished. For sins separate us from God; as He Himself speaks; \"Have not your sins separated between you and Me? But punishments lead us back to God. As one saith, \"Give peace; for Thou hast recompensed us for all things. Suppose any one hath a wound; which is the most deserving of fear, gangrene, or the surgeon's knife? the steel, or the devouring progress of the ulcer? Sin is a gangrene, punishment is the surgeon's knife. …"],[2,null,0,"An Exhortation to Theodore After His Fall","Letter 1",1,407,"For He Himself never turns away from us, but it is we who put ourselves far off: for \"I am a God\" we read \"at hand and not a God afar off. And again, when He was rebuking them by the mouth of this prophet He said \"Do not your sins separate between you and me? Inasmuch then as this is the cause which puts us far from God, let us remove this obnoxious barrier, which prevents any near approach being made. But now hear how this has actually occurred in real instances. Amongst the Corinthians some man of mark committed a sin such as was not named even among the Gentiles. …"],[5,null,1,"Against Eunomius","Book 2",2,395,"Of such a kind as this, perhaps, is that of which the prophet says, touching the ungodly, \"They weave a spider's web. For as in the cobweb there is the appearance of something woven, but no substantiality in the appearance,—for he who touches it touches nothing substantial, as the spider's threads break with the touch of a finger,—just such is the unsubstantial texture of idle phrases. \"Not dividing His own essence by begetting and being at once begetter and begotten. …"]],"fau":[["chrysostom","John Chrysostom",1,"father",null,1,407],["gregory-nyssa","Gregory of Nyssa",0,"father",null,1,395]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 13 (1889)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf113"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 9 (1889)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf109"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 5 (1893)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf205"]]}